WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.453
(audience cheers)
- You got your own couch.

00:00:05.427 --> 00:00:07.223
You got your very own couch.

00:00:07.223 --> 00:00:09.150
- I know.
- And we complimented

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:10.470
your outfit with the colors.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:12.021
- I know.
- It wasn't even planned.

00:00:12.021 --> 00:00:12.854
- [Rachel] It was planned.

00:00:12.854 --> 00:00:14.430
- Oh my gosh. Welcome
to the show everybody.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:16.530
- Thank you.
(audience cheers)

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.600
- All right, so what was
your first experience

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:19.433
with "The Hunger Games"?

00:00:19.433 --> 00:00:20.266
'Cause y'all are quite young

00:00:20.266 --> 00:00:22.290
and I remember reading
the books a long time ago.

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:23.550
So what was your first experience?

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:26.190
- Yeah, mine, I didn't
read the books originally,

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:27.450
which ended up being a blessing on this,

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.160
so I didn't get ahead of myself.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:31.260
But I went to see them
with my family growing up,

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:32.340
every weekend they came out,

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.640
and then we went to the London premiere

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.680
a couple of weeks ago,
a couple of days ago,

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.650
God, time is like a whirlwind in my head.

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.510
And I got to introduce
the film and look up,

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:43.500
and they were in the back row

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.659
and got to kind of dedicate--

00:00:44.659 --> 00:00:45.600
- Oh, your family? Oh!
- Dedicate it to them.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.010
So it was like a full circle moment.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:49.410
- That's super cool. What
was your first experience?

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:50.640
- Similarly to Tom,

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.290
it was kind of a bonding experience

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:53.940
for my mom, my sister, and myself.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:55.140
And we would always go

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.480
to the Barnes and Noble
releases of the books.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:59.910
And then the Thursday night
screenings of the movies.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:03.030
And so, this whole experience
has been very surreal

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:05.880
and strange and weird. (laughs)

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:06.960
- What about you, Josh?

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.740
- Yeah, well I was a terrible student.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:12.573
(audience laughs)

00:01:12.573 --> 00:01:14.589
- Lead with that. Okay. (laughs)

00:01:14.589 --> 00:01:17.280
- There was a semester
I had pretty bad grades,

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.050
so I had to give up a lunch period

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:22.050
so I could go to the library
and like do study hall.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:23.970
We had a really good librarian there

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:26.640
that gave me "The Hunger Games" book.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.500
No, he was lovely.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:29.790
He gave me "The Hunger Games" book

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:30.780
and he was like, "Read this,

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:32.130
and I promise reading's fun.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:34.177
It's like a good thing that," you know,

00:01:34.177 --> 00:01:36.600
"it's good for your brain and it's fun."

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.010
So yeah. So then I read it.

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:39.360
And it was, I mean, to his credit,

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.820
it was the first book I ever
read that I couldn't put down.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:43.589
And it really kind of jump-started

00:01:43.589 --> 00:01:45.290
recreational reading for me.
- Just takes that, right?

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:47.340
Yeah.
- Yeah, so it's kind of,

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:50.851
it's got a formative place with me, so.

00:01:50.851 --> 00:01:51.690
- Oh, that's so cool.

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:52.782
Now you're in the movie.

00:01:52.782 --> 00:01:53.615
(audience laughs)

00:01:53.615 --> 00:01:54.448
Hunter, what about you?

00:01:54.448 --> 00:01:58.290
- I just remember it being
like the cool book to read

00:01:58.290 --> 00:01:59.670
in school, you know?

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:01.770
In middle school.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:04.530
So I ended up picking it up

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:07.950
and just became obsessed immediately.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:09.510
Yeah, and went through all three.

00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:12.510
And then, yeah, and the
movies started filming

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:14.340
in North Carolina too,
which is where I was.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:15.494
So we were like,

00:02:15.494 --> 00:02:16.327
- [Kelly] Oh, right on.

00:02:16.327 --> 00:02:17.160
- hearing about it filming.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:18.657
And we were like, (squeals)
we should go be extras!

00:02:18.657 --> 00:02:20.010
- Did you ever, I was gonna say,

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:21.000
did you ever try be an extra?

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.350
- No, but we wanted to.

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:24.360
- Oh, well you skipped extra, didn't you?

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:25.460
There you go.
- Yeah!

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:26.627
There you go.

00:02:26.627 --> 00:02:28.290
So Rachel, I found this crazy.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:30.840
So you actually had to turn
down the role at first, right?

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:31.770
- I did.
- Yeah.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:34.440
- I had just moved out to
London when I met Francis

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.360
for the first time, our director.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:38.310
And he was so keen on me doing the part,

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:39.143
and it was such an honor

00:02:39.143 --> 00:02:41.820
'cause I was such a fan of
the book and of the character.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:44.580
And when he said that we
were shooting in Poland

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:47.550
and in Germany, and I had only
been in London for two weeks

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:49.230
and I missed my family

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:50.100
and my friends and everything.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:50.933
- Yeah, stability.

00:02:50.933 --> 00:02:52.260
- He said that my face turned white

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:54.352
and he knew immediately
I wasn't gonna do it.

00:02:54.352 --> 00:02:57.600
And then I kind of caught my
second wind working out there.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:01.200
I made a lot of friends and
I felt like I could do it.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:04.020
And I felt confident in
my ability to carry on

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:06.570
for the next six months without my family.

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:08.190
And I'm really glad I did it.

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:09.300
I'm really glad I changed my mind.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:10.133
- That's incredible.

00:03:10.133 --> 00:03:12.540
First, I mean, not to down youth,

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.100
I just mean it's incredible
for someone so young,

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:14.940
I was so stupid.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:16.350
I said yes to everything.

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:17.183
Like, you know what I'm saying?

00:03:17.183 --> 00:03:19.140
Like, even when I was worn
out, walking pneumonia,

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:20.490
I still was like, "Okay, I can do it.

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:21.323
I can do it."

00:03:21.323 --> 00:03:23.220
That's so smart. I found this crazy.

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:24.690
But I didn't know y'all
were dating before,

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:27.270
but you, did you know that
she had turned down the role

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:29.580
whenever you accepted the
role, like you got the role?

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:30.900
Did you know that?
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:32.460
I mean we had talked about it

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.860
'cause that happened a lot

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:36.166
further,
- Earlier, yeah.

00:03:36.166 --> 00:03:37.134
- earlier than--

00:03:37.134 --> 00:03:37.967
- Than when you started auditioning.

00:03:37.967 --> 00:03:39.870
- Yeah, 'cause I started auditioning,

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:41.640
by the time I started auditioning,

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:43.829
it was a pretty quick turnaround.

00:03:43.829 --> 00:03:47.910
Like I sent in the tape and
then I got the callback.

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:50.430
And then a couple days after
that they offered me the role.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:51.900
And it was really one of those things

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:54.420
where I was like, no way, you know?

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:55.560
Like I didn't, I don't know.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:59.490
It's such a huge revered
piece of like pop culture.

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:00.660
I was like, "Oh, this is cool.

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:02.700
Maybe they'll see me and
they'll put me in something else

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:03.690
that they're working on," you know?

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:04.680
But they gave me the role.
- Yeah, that's smart too

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:06.000
though, yeah.
- And then I pretty much

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:06.960
immediately called Rachel.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:08.520
I was like, "That thing
that you were gonna do,

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:09.569
I'm gonna do it."

00:04:09.569 --> 00:04:11.552
(audience laughs)

00:04:11.552 --> 00:04:12.385
So--

00:04:12.385 --> 00:04:13.590
- And then she was like, "Well wait."

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:14.820
- I'm pretty sure we ended up

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:17.760
like officially saying
yes on the same day too.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:20.250
'Cause I remember that
FaceTime call, yeah.

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:22.320
- [Kelly] Oh, that's
cute. Oh, that's cool.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:23.340
- Yeah.

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:25.950
- But I heard that you
had, wasn't it like a very,

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:27.090
no, no, I'm sorry, it was you.

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:29.130
You had the interesting chemistry read.

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:30.030
- Oh!
- Whenever,

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:31.770
because a heat wave was happening

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:33.300
and you had no AC and you were (chuckles).

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:34.970
- So I live in Brooklyn and there was a,

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:36.330
it was last, when was it?

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:38.130
Last summer? Beginning of last summer.

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:40.830
Yeah, and there was this
massive, unprecedented heat wave.

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:43.917
And I lived in a little
shoebox apartment in New York

00:04:43.917 --> 00:04:44.750
as you do,
- As you do.

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:45.660
- as you do when you live there.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:47.610
And I had these old AC units

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:49.830
that all just broke the
day of our chemistry read.

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:51.930
And I'd been told I already had the job,

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:55.530
but I went into that
audition so sweaty, literally

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:59.550
and metaphorically,
sweaty, and emotionally,

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:01.200
that and I went in, I was
like, "I'm gonna lose it.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.060
I'm gonna lose the job
that I've already got."

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:04.530
- Because of the heat wave.
- Because I'm just sweating,

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:06.000
I'm dripping, I can't remember the lines.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:07.040
I'm like (mumbles).

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:07.873
- 'Cause you're on Zoom

00:05:07.873 --> 00:05:09.027
and you're like trying to keep it cool.

00:05:09.027 --> 00:05:09.860
- Yeah, I'm trying to keep it cool

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:10.797
and collected
- Beads of sweat.

00:05:10.797 --> 00:05:12.630
and you know, I've already
jumped through all the hoops

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:13.463
there are to jump.

00:05:13.463 --> 00:05:14.550
So I was like, I've got this.

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:15.690
And then I thought I hadn't.

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:17.310
And then the minute we got on together,

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:19.050
it was like instant magic

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:20.760
and Rachel sang and suddenly I forgot--

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:22.356
- Did she lean in and
start sweating with you?

00:05:22.356 --> 00:05:23.189
Just to be nice.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:23.189 --> 00:05:24.900
That's how empathetic an
actor Rachel Zegler is.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:26.520
- Empathetic sweating.
- Like she saw the beads

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:27.390
and she just beads herself
- I've got you, boo.

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:29.619
- of sweat. (laughs)

00:05:29.619 --> 00:05:30.827
- That's so sad.

00:05:30.827 --> 00:05:32.880
And of course like just the universe,

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:34.710
you're like, sure, today.

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:36.780
Can you explain, everybody,
I'll go down the row,

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:38.280
but like explain your characters.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:40.170
- Yeah, so I played Coriolanus Snow,

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:42.750
who fans may remember from
the original franchise films.

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:43.920
- [Kelly] Horrible man.

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:46.470
- Horrible then, 64 years later.

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:48.090
I play him as an 18-year-old.

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:51.360
So he is a lot younger and
a little more innocent,

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:52.193
at least at the beginning.

00:05:52.193 --> 00:05:54.600
And then he meets Lucy Gray
and his heart is on the line

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:56.250
and that challenges
everything he thinks he knows

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:58.590
about control and power and by the end,

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:00.720
he's a very, very different man.

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:02.070
- Hell yeah, he is.

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:03.550
- [Tom] Hell yeah.
(audience laughs)

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:05.280
- Yeah, what about your character?

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:06.900
- I'm Lucy Gray Baird.

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:09.990
I am the girl tribute from District 12

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:13.290
and I get assigned a mentor in Coriolanus,

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:16.110
and there's a bit of
a romantic connection,

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:18.270
but with a lot of asterisks after that

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:21.150
where you try to figure out
if she's clinging on for love

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:23.310
or self-preservation or both.

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:25.020
And there's a really cool dynamic

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:28.530
between her and in the
games, and she also sings.

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:29.880
So there's that.

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:31.878
- I know, I love the
singing in this, obviously.

00:06:31.878 --> 00:06:33.330
I'm a fan.

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:34.830
Well Josh, tell everybody
about your character.

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:36.900
- Yeah. I play Sejanus Plinth.

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:38.970
He is one of the mentors,

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:41.130
but originally he's from the districts.

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:44.370
So he has a real hard time reckoning

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:45.203
with "The Hunger Games".

00:06:45.203 --> 00:06:46.650
He's very vocally against it.

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:49.726
He's kind of the moral
compass of the plot.

00:06:49.726 --> 00:06:53.130
And he meets a lot of
adversity because of it.

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:54.970
- Have you've done that before?
Have you told someone that?

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:58.144
That was a very good synopsis.
- Once or twice.

00:06:58.144 --> 00:06:59.550
- Yes. Hunter, what about you?

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:04.260
- I play Tigris Snow, who is
Coriolanus' cousin technically,

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:07.770
but they're kind of more
like brother, sister,

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:10.290
also kind of like mother
father for each other

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:12.810
because they've been
taking care of each other

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:15.690
since they were wee little children.

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:17.730
So yeah.
- Yeah.

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:19.380
I love, yeah, I love y'all's dynamic.

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:21.480
So wait, you two actually
bonded, Hunter and Tom,

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:22.313
off screen, right?

00:07:22.313 --> 00:07:24.090
Y'all go like dancing, right?

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:25.680
- I mean, Berlin.

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:27.000
- We were in Berlin and--

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:27.840
- [Kelly] Went to some clubs.

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:30.270
- Hunter and I have a shared
love of moving to music

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:31.103
a little bit.

00:07:31.103 --> 00:07:32.070
So she's way better than I am.

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:33.990
- I was gonna say, you
called it moving to music,

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:35.340
which means leads me to believe maybe,

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:36.517
you're not a great dancer.
- Moving to music, yes.

00:07:36.517 --> 00:07:37.588
Exactly.

00:07:37.588 --> 00:07:39.658
In my head, I'm just
like move to the beat.

00:07:39.658 --> 00:07:40.491
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:07:40.491 --> 00:07:41.324
And Hunter's way better.
- That's awesome.

00:07:41.324 --> 00:07:42.510
- But I learned a few moves from her,

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:43.470
you know, she's great.

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:45.330
- That's awesome. Just to get to hang out.

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:46.950
I love Berlin. Did you love it?

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:48.120
- Oh my God, it's the best.

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:52.080
And I love techno so much.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:55.410
And, it is also great
because you can kind of,

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:56.490
it's a space where you don't,

00:07:56.490 --> 00:07:58.080
it's not like you dance with other people.

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:00.870
Like you can really be in your own zone,

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:05.130
but every once in a while you
share a moment with somebody.

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:07.061
And there's like this one moment,

00:08:07.061 --> 00:08:11.700
I think where I just
remember looking over to Tom

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:14.580
and then he looks at me and
he's got the biggest smile

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:16.110
I've ever seen on his face.

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:17.712
And he's going.

00:08:17.712 --> 00:08:18.545
- This is great.

00:08:18.545 --> 00:08:21.210
(everyone laughs)

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:22.043
He's moving.

00:08:22.043 --> 00:08:23.370
- Yeah.
- Oh no.

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:24.688
- He's moving.
- He's moving to the music.

00:08:24.688 --> 00:08:26.110
- That is me moving to the music.

00:08:26.110 --> 00:08:26.943
(everyone laughs)

00:08:26.943 --> 00:08:29.023
- I love this song.

00:08:29.023 --> 00:08:30.960
It's like, I've always wanted to do those,

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:32.430
I don't know if you've ever done it.

00:08:32.430 --> 00:08:33.750
You put headphones on, they do it

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:35.334
a lot of house music.
- Silent disco!

00:08:35.334 --> 00:08:36.167
- Yes!

00:08:36.167 --> 00:08:37.080
- That's a big thing in England.

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:38.250
I don't know why,
- I've really always wanted

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:39.083
to do it!
- but we love

00:08:39.083 --> 00:08:39.916
the silent disco.

00:08:39.916 --> 00:08:41.040
I think it's 'cause
we're all very inhibited,

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:42.057
embarrassed people, so we can just--

00:08:42.057 --> 00:08:42.890
- I'm not,

00:08:42.890 --> 00:08:44.400
I'm a very open person.
- No, in England, I mean.

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:45.233
- No, no, but I'm saying,

00:08:45.233 --> 00:08:47.970
but I would love it because
I think it's kind of nice

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:49.020
because sometimes it's annoying,

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:50.700
you go out dancing and
then someone's annoying you

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:52.680
and you're like, I didn't
come here to dance with you.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:53.790
I just came to dance with me.

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:55.160
- Came here to move to the music.

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:57.656
- Yeah, to move like this, like Tom.

00:08:57.656 --> 00:08:59.163
So there we go. Uh, uh.

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:01.230
It's like a fast one.

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:02.460
I've always wanted to do that though.

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:04.560
I'm so jealous that y'all
have done that. Oh my gosh.

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:06.630
Anyway, so I found this funny.

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:07.950
I didn't know that y'all were dating

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:10.800
and then I found out that
you actually passed notes,

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:14.010
this is your thing, which I
think is kind of incredible

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:16.133
and so super cute high school.

00:09:16.133 --> 00:09:19.410
But you pass notes on set.

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:20.243
- Yeah.

00:09:20.243 --> 00:09:22.710
- Not just to you, like,
you just, you pass notes.

00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:23.670
Yeah.

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:24.900
- Yeah, yeah.

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:28.495
Well I was doodling one day
in my little academy notebook.

00:09:28.495 --> 00:09:30.780
- [Kelly] You just slide
it in? You just like.

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:32.280
- Yeah.
- There he goes.

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:34.650
- [Josh] So Rachel, as you can see, she--

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:37.120
- [Rachel] I think it's
something cute and then...

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:38.130
(everyone laughs)

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:39.397
- [Kelly] You're like, "Aww."

00:09:39.397 --> 00:09:40.230
- [Rachel] Something silly.

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:43.680
- She told me to draw her as a flower.

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:47.280
So, you know, I did this really
crude drawing of this flower

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:50.332
with a little smiley
face on it and some legs

00:09:50.332 --> 00:09:52.200
and like just handed it to her.

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:56.220
But you know what, I'm glad
that that became such a story.

00:09:56.220 --> 00:09:58.740
'Cause afterwards I got
kind of into drawing.

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:00.540
I feel like I got a lot better.

00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:02.220
- He's really harnessed his ability.

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:04.710
- Yeah, and I actually made you something

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:05.543
earlier.
- What?

00:10:05.543 --> 00:10:07.950
- Yes, I found out that your
favorite animal is a dog.

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:09.570
You really like dogs, right?
- I love dogs.

00:10:09.570 --> 00:10:11.640
- So in the same vein of of this,

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:13.618
I decided to draw you as a dog.

00:10:13.618 --> 00:10:14.460
- Did you draw me as a dog?

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:16.710
- [Rachel] It's amazing. He's so talented.

00:10:16.710 --> 00:10:18.533
Make sure you show--
- So this is kind of...

00:10:18.533 --> 00:10:21.855
(audience laughs)

00:10:21.855 --> 00:10:24.855
(audience applauds)

00:10:26.697 --> 00:10:28.457
- [Rachel] You have to explain it.

00:10:28.457 --> 00:10:29.670
(Josh stammers)

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:31.260
- Like you're such a great team,

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:32.970
she set that up so well.

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:34.167
I was like, okay.
- He told me before.

00:10:34.167 --> 00:10:36.120
- So, but there's a lot
of nuance to this drawing,

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:38.730
as you see also 'cause you're a singer,

00:10:38.730 --> 00:10:40.110
so that you're barking, there's little--

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:41.250
- [Kelly] It is in a musical note.

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:42.397
- [Josh] Yes, that's right.

00:10:42.397 --> 00:10:43.879
- [Tom] Kelly, you know the
really scary part of this?

00:10:43.879 --> 00:10:45.925
The really scary part is
this is after he got better.

00:10:45.925 --> 00:10:47.430
- This is after he got better?
(audience laughs)

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:48.450
Well Tom, I didn't know this,

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:50.970
but Julliard, you went to
Julliard just like Viola Davis.

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:51.803
- Yes.

00:10:51.803 --> 00:10:55.170
- Who was who by the way
is so scary in this movie.

00:10:55.170 --> 00:10:56.820
That is like, how she said the word dull.

00:10:56.820 --> 00:10:58.440
I almost peed my pants.

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:00.243
I was like, don't piss her off.

00:11:00.243 --> 00:11:02.865
Anyway, but I didn't know
that you both went there.

00:11:02.865 --> 00:11:04.080
I knew she went there, but I
didn't know you did as well.

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:04.913
That's amazing. Did y'all--

00:11:04.913 --> 00:11:06.235
- Yeah, we weren't in the same class.

00:11:06.235 --> 00:11:07.320
- Obviously.
(audience laughs)

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:08.370
- Different generations.

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:09.330
But I didn't know that
you both went there.

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:10.927
I just thought that was
kind of a cool thing.

00:11:10.927 --> 00:11:11.820
- We did.
- That's an amazing thing

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:14.100
that you did in your life
that you both have in common.

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:15.630
- Yeah, I mean it's the
reason I ended up in New York,

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:17.310
which I'm very grateful for.

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:18.600
But it's kind of how we bonded on set

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:20.670
was like talking about, you
know, our shared stories

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:21.503
and weirdly found out

00:11:21.503 --> 00:11:23.610
that we have like some of
the same teachers years apart

00:11:23.610 --> 00:11:24.650
that have stayed there.

00:11:24.650 --> 00:11:25.483
- Really? I was gonna ask that.

00:11:25.483 --> 00:11:26.610
- Yeah, one of our
movement coaches in school

00:11:26.610 --> 00:11:27.630
was the same teacher,

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:30.240
so he'd just been there his
entire existence I guess.

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:32.430
- Wow. That's really cool.

00:11:32.430 --> 00:11:33.510
- Yeah.
- Awesome.

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:35.490
Did you ever actually get
to meet Jennifer Lawrence?

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:36.323
Anybody here for that?

00:11:36.323 --> 00:11:37.156
- I did.

00:11:37.156 --> 00:11:38.537
I think I'm the only one. I did.

00:11:38.537 --> 00:11:41.747
We were at the Paris Fashion
Week for the Dior show

00:11:41.747 --> 00:11:43.890
only a couple weeks ago.

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:45.990
And, and I didn't want to be weird

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:48.853
and I was just kind of psyching
myself up to talk to her

00:11:48.853 --> 00:11:51.270
and she was so unbelievably kind.

00:11:51.270 --> 00:11:53.250
And in that clip she's actually saying,

00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:55.440
let's do another photo
where I'm strangling you,

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:57.305
saying you'll never take my part.

00:11:57.305 --> 00:11:59.687
(everyone laughs)

00:11:59.687 --> 00:12:02.953
And that is exactly
what I wanted from her.

00:12:02.953 --> 00:12:03.960
- I love that about her.

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:06.690
She seems like the same
offscreen and onscreen.

00:12:06.690 --> 00:12:07.950
- Truly.
- That's amazing.

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:09.180
That's a cool thing, like working with,

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:11.010
there's so many people in this movie too,

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:13.710
like Viola, that's just amazing
to be able to work with too.

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:15.880
Do you get nervous in scenes
with people like that?

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:18.150
- Yeah, I mean, especially
when you see Viola's costume,

00:12:18.150 --> 00:12:19.320
when she comes in with that fake iron.

00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:20.153
- Dude, she's scared--

00:12:20.153 --> 00:12:22.380
- Everything. It's kind of terrifying.

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:23.700
But then she breaks for lunch

00:12:23.700 --> 00:12:25.350
and she's the funniest
person in the world.

00:12:25.350 --> 00:12:26.430
We were just with her this morning.

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:28.110
She's so funny.
- She's so funny.

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:30.630
- She immediately cuts
through any intimidation.

00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:32.028
She's great.
- Oh, I love that.

00:12:32.028 --> 00:12:32.861
Awesome.
- Yeah.

00:12:32.861 --> 00:12:34.200
- Well, what was your
reaction to the film,

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:35.070
like when you first saw it?

00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:35.970
I'll start with you.

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:38.640
- Yeah, I mean I was kind
of, I was blown away.

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:40.020
I have this thing when I see myself

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:41.310
where I don't believe it's me.

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:42.360
Like I think it's someone else.

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:43.890
I have like a total disassociation

00:12:43.890 --> 00:12:45.197
from the person on the screen.
- It's 'cause you're good.

00:12:45.197 --> 00:12:46.260
That's good.
- Thank you.

00:12:46.260 --> 00:12:47.594
I think it's 'cause I'm mental.

00:12:47.594 --> 00:12:48.780
(everybody laughs)

00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:50.031
- [Kelly] But we're gonna go with good.

00:12:50.031 --> 00:12:50.864
- We're gonna go with good.

00:12:50.864 --> 00:12:51.697
- Unpack that later.

00:12:51.697 --> 00:12:52.761
- [Tom] Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:12:52.761 --> 00:12:53.594
What about you?

00:12:53.594 --> 00:12:55.740
- I was just, I mean
there's this sense of relief

00:12:55.740 --> 00:12:58.110
even if you never had a shadow of a doubt

00:12:58.110 --> 00:12:59.460
that the movie was gonna be good.

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:01.290
But we filmed for such a long time

00:13:01.290 --> 00:13:03.570
and this movie is
separated into three parts

00:13:03.570 --> 00:13:04.890
within the movie.

00:13:04.890 --> 00:13:07.020
And it felt like we were
filming three different movies

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:07.853
while we were working.

00:13:07.853 --> 00:13:09.960
'Cause it felt like coming
of age, high school,

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:12.750
also the Hunger Games,
also war, also this,

00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:14.670
and all of a sudden it just,

00:13:14.670 --> 00:13:16.410
it came together in such a beautiful way

00:13:16.410 --> 00:13:17.460
and it was just kind of this,

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:20.940
oh, this is a really special
thing we have on our hands.

00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:21.840
- Oh, that's cool.
- We saw it together,

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:22.673
what did you think?

00:13:22.673 --> 00:13:24.225
- Yeah, well, I mean,

00:13:24.225 --> 00:13:26.700
I don't know if you
remember my first reaction,

00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:28.950
but like, I think I watched the movie,

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:30.990
the credits were rolling,
and I sat there for a second

00:13:30.990 --> 00:13:34.977
and I was like, "I think
I'm gonna keep working."

00:13:36.852 --> 00:13:38.967
- Aw, I love that.

00:13:38.967 --> 00:13:40.950
(audience applauds)

00:13:40.950 --> 00:13:43.740
That's good because it looks
so great. It was so great.

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:45.360
It's something that people
are really gonna get into.

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:47.130
Yeah. That's awesome.

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:48.750
What about you, Hunter?

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:50.940
- The first time I saw the screening,

00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:54.330
I know it happened because
it was with you guys,

00:13:54.330 --> 00:13:58.358
but I complete, I have no
recollection of seeing it.

00:13:58.358 --> 00:13:59.430
(everyone laughs)

00:13:59.430 --> 00:14:02.850
I don't know, I think maybe
I went out the night before

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:04.740
and we were just like, still going.

00:14:04.740 --> 00:14:06.030
- [Tom] Were you moving to music?

00:14:06.030 --> 00:14:06.863
- What?

00:14:06.863 --> 00:14:07.696
- Were you moving to music?

00:14:07.696 --> 00:14:08.610
- Doing this with Tom.
- Were you doing Tom's move?

00:14:08.610 --> 00:14:10.111
Were you utilizing--

00:14:10.111 --> 00:14:10.944
- I don't know, I don't remember.

00:14:10.944 --> 00:14:13.800
But I had to set up a second
screening a few weeks ago

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:14.850
before press started

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:18.150
because I had literally no
recollection of seeing it.

00:14:18.150 --> 00:14:19.470
But I was there, right?
- All I have to say,

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:22.020
is what an amazing night before.

00:14:22.020 --> 00:14:24.360
Like that must have been
an incredible night before,

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:27.486
if it has the aftermath
of not remembering.

00:14:27.486 --> 00:14:28.634
- Yeah. I'm not insane.

00:14:28.634 --> 00:14:30.720
- Yeah.

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:32.100
Well, and also it's, I don't know,

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:34.440
it would be weird to see
yourself in something so epic.

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:35.730
Like something so big too.

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:38.347
I would be a little removed
from that I think too.

00:14:38.347 --> 00:14:39.903
"Billy The Kid," there's
a new season, right?

00:14:39.903 --> 00:14:40.736
- Yes. Yeah.

00:14:40.736 --> 00:14:43.500
The first half of season two aired

00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:45.040
the beginning of the winter this year.

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:47.490
We're we're going back to film
the second half of season two

00:14:47.490 --> 00:14:48.390
because of the SAG strike.

00:14:48.390 --> 00:14:49.680
We got shut down.

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:52.710
So that will be out early next year.

00:14:52.710 --> 00:14:55.290
It's a really fun job. I
really like riding horses.

00:14:55.290 --> 00:14:56.670
- I was gonna say, are
you a natural on a horse?

00:14:56.670 --> 00:14:57.503
Do you like it?

00:14:57.503 --> 00:14:59.340
- So I first started
riding when I was a kid,

00:14:59.340 --> 00:15:01.380
fell off after two lessons
and didn't get back on.

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:04.350
And then I started again two
years ago and now I love it.

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:05.880
Oh, there I am with Bill.
- Boy with a horse.

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:07.080
- That guy.
- That's my horse, Bill.

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:07.913
He has a weepy eye.

00:15:07.913 --> 00:15:08.760
- [Group] Aw!

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:11.040
- He's like perpetually
crying for some reason.

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:11.873
And it's not because of me,

00:15:11.873 --> 00:15:12.706
I promise.
- Josh should draw him.

00:15:12.706 --> 00:15:15.090
- Yeah, Josh, next challenge, Josh

00:15:15.090 --> 00:15:17.999
is draw me with Billy.
- He kinda did with my dog.

00:15:17.999 --> 00:15:18.832
- Yeah, wasn't that what that was?

00:15:18.832 --> 00:15:20.253
It's kind of a blanket animal.

